    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Every other crack in a driveway is a crack in the asphalt. Alligator cracking is a crack in whatever is under the asphalt. Treat it like the others and it comes back within a year, every time.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. What Alligator Cracking Actually Is
        </h2>

        <p>
          Alligator cracking, also called fatigue cracking or crocodile cracking depending on which side of the Atlantic your paving contractor trained on, is a network of short interconnected cracks that divide the surface into small angular pieces. The pieces are usually two to six inches across and the pattern genuinely does look like reptile skin. It is one of the few driveway problems that everyone recognises on sight, and one of the few that is almost universally misdiagnosed as a surface issue.
        </p>

        <p>
          Asphalt is a flexible pavement. Unlike concrete, it is designed to bend a little under a wheel and spring back, and it does that happily for decades provided two things are true: the layer of compacted stone underneath it is stiff enough to limit how far it bends, and the asphalt binder is still supple enough to tolerate the bending. Alligator cracking appears when one or both of those conditions stop being true.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Base Failure</h3>
        <p>
          The most common cause on residential drives is the base. Water gets under the asphalt, through cracks, from a lawn that slopes toward the drive, from a downspout that discharges onto the edge, or simply from a base that was never thick enough or compacted properly when the drive was built. Wet aggregate loses bearing strength. Clay subgrade turns to something with the consistency of cold porridge. The asphalt above then deflects far more under each wheel than it was ever meant to, and after enough load cycles it fatigues and breaks into pieces. That is why alligator cracking so often shows up in the wheel paths, near the garage apron where cars sit and turn, and along edges where there is no lateral support.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Aged, Brittle Asphalt</h3>
        <p>
          The second cause is the asphalt itself. Bitumen oxidises. Sun and oxygen strip out the lighter oils over the years and the binder hardens, which is why a fifteen-year-old drive is grey and a new one is black. Brittle asphalt has very little fatigue life left, so even a base that was adequate when the drive was new can no longer keep the deflection within what the surface will tolerate. On an old, never-sealed drive you often get both problems together: a base that has taken on water through years of open cracks, under a surface that has lost its flexibility.
        </p>

        <p>
          The practical consequence is the same in both cases. The pieces are loose. Press on them with your boot and they move independently of each other. Water gets down between them, the base gets wetter, the pieces move more, and the area grows outward season by season. Left alone, the pieces start to rock, the edges round off, and the area turns into a pothole. Alligator cracking is, quite literally, the stage before a pothole.
        </p>

        <h2 id="not-alligator" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. Cracks That Look Similar but Are Not
        </h2>

        <p>
          Getting the diagnosis right matters because the correct repair for the other common crack patterns is cheaper and far less invasive. We see a lot of homeowners who have been told they need a new driveway when what they actually have is shrinkage cracking that wants a tube of filler and a coat of sealer.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Block Cracking</h3>
        <p>
          Block cracking divides the surface into large, roughly rectangular pieces, typically a foot to several feet across, and it appears across the whole drive rather than in patches. It is caused by the asphalt shrinking as it ages and has nothing to do with the base. The blocks are solid underfoot and do not rock. Block cracking is annoying and lets water in, but it is a crack-fill-and-seal job, not a structural one. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-fill-large-cracks-in-asphalt-driveway/">filling large cracks in asphalt</a> covers it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Shrinkage and Thermal Cracks</h3>
        <p>
          Long single cracks running across the drive, often at fairly regular spacing, are thermal cracks. They open in winter as the asphalt contracts and close somewhat in summer. They are not connected into a network and the asphalt on either side is sound. A good rubberised crack filler handles these, and the <a href="/best-asphalt-crack-filler/">best asphalt crack fillers</a> are formulated precisely for the seasonal movement.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Edge Cracks</h3>
        <p>
          Cracks running parallel to the edge of the drive, a foot or so in from the side, are edge cracks. They come from a lack of side support, usually because the lawn or gravel next to the drive has settled or been eroded away, leaving the outer edge of the asphalt unsupported. Edge cracks can develop into alligatoring along the edge if ignored, but on their own the fix is to fill the crack and rebuild the shoulder with compacted topsoil or crushed stone up to the level of the asphalt.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Crumbling and Ravelling</h3>
        <p>
          Ravelling is loose surface aggregate coming away, leaving a rough, pitted texture, and it is the result of a worn or oxidised binder rather than base movement. It often appears alongside alligatoring on an old drive but it is a different failure with a different remedy, covered in our piece on <a href="/how-to-fix-crumbling-asphalt-driveway/">fixing a crumbling asphalt driveway</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          The quick field test that separates all of these from true alligator cracking: stand on the cracked area and shift your weight. Sound asphalt with cracks in it does not move. Alligatored asphalt does, even if only slightly. If you can see water squeeze up between the pieces after rain, or a puddle forms there because the area has settled, you have a base problem and no amount of surface work will change that.
        </p>

        <h2 id="assessment" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. The Honest Assessment
        </h2>

        <p>
          Before buying anything, walk the whole drive and map the alligatored areas. Chalk around them if it helps. What you are trying to establish is three things: how much of the drive is affected, how bad each area is, and why it happened. The answers decide which of the four repair routes below makes sense, and there is no point spending a weekend on a gator patch if the honest answer is that the drive is finished.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Much</h3>
        <p>
          A couple of areas each under about twenty square feet, on a drive that is otherwise sound, is a patch job and a good DIY one. Several areas adding up to more than roughly a quarter of the surface, or a continuous band the length of a wheel path, is not. At that point the base has failed generally rather than locally, and the money is better spent on an overlay or replacement. We have watched people spend three summers chasing spreading alligatoring around a drive with patch products when a single overlay at the start would have cost about the same and lasted fifteen years.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Bad</h3>
        <p>
          Grade each area. Stage one is a tight network of hairline cracks, no movement, pieces still level. Stage two is wider cracks, slight movement underfoot, perhaps a little settlement. Stage three is rocking pieces, rounded edges, missing chunks, a depression that holds water. Stage one and early stage two respond well to a surface patch. Late stage two and stage three need the failed material removed and the base dealt with, because the surface is no longer stable enough for anything applied over it to survive.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why</h3>
        <p>
          This is the part most people skip. If the alligatoring is where the downspout discharges, fix the downspout or the patch will fail the same way. If it is at the low corner where the lawn drains onto the drive, regrade or add a channel. If it is where a heavy vehicle regularly parks, either the base was never designed for that load or the patch needs to be a proper full-depth repair. If it is everywhere and the drive is twenty-plus years old, the cause is simply age and there is nothing to fix at the source; the surface has reached the end of its life.
        </p>

        <h2 id="gator-patch" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. Option A: Gator Patch, Step by Step
        </h2>

        <p>
          For small, stable alligatored areas, the trowel-grade alligator patch products are genuinely useful and this is the repair most homeowners should attempt first. Latex-ite Gator Patch is the one you will find at Home Depot and Lowe's; Gardner's Gator-Pave and a few contractor-grade equivalents from Sealmaster and Neyra do the same job. They are thick, fibre-reinforced acrylic or asphalt-emulsion compounds that you spread across the cracked area in a thin layer. They bridge the network of cracks, bond the loose pieces together into a single skin, and give the sealer a sound surface to sit on. They do not fix the base. They buy time, and on a stable area that time can be several years.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Tools and Materials</h3>
        <p>
          A stiff broom, a wire brush or a flat scraper, a leaf blower or shop vac, a garden hose or pressure washer, a weed killer, a tube or pour bottle of rubberised crack filler for any cracks wider than a quarter inch, a wide flexible trowel or a squeegee with a firm rubber blade, a margin trowel for edges, gloves and eye protection, and the patch itself. A one-gallon tub of Gator Patch covers roughly ten to fifteen square feet at the thickness you want; a 3.5-gallon pail does around forty to fifty. Buy slightly more than the maths says, because the cracked areas soak up more than the flat spots.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Step 1: Pick the Weather</h3>
        <p>
          You want a surface temperature of at least 55 degrees Fahrenheit and rising, no rain forecast for 24 hours, and ideally a dry day following a dry day so the cracks are not holding water. Direct summer sun on black asphalt can push the surface to 130 degrees, at which point the patch skins over before you can feather it, so early morning or late afternoon on a hot day is better than midday. Our guide on <a href="/what-temperature-to-seal-driveway/">what temperature to seal a driveway</a> applies equally to patching.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Step 2: Clean the Area Properly</h3>
        <p>
          The patch is only ever as good as its bond, and it will not bond to dust, dead grass, old flaking sealer or oil. Scrape out anything loose between the pieces with the wire brush or scraper. Sweep hard, then blow or vacuum the cracks out, because a broom leaves fine grit in the bottom of every crack. If there is oil staining, degrease it and rinse. A pressure washer on a fan tip is ideal for getting the cracks properly clean, but you then have to wait for the area to dry completely, which in a shaded spot can take a full day. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-clean-asphalt-driveway-before-sealing/">cleaning asphalt before sealing</a> goes into detail.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Step 3: Kill the Vegetation</h3>
        <p>
          Weeds and grass growing up through alligator cracks are more than cosmetic. Roots hold moisture in the base and they will grow straight up through a patch. Pull what you can, then treat the cracks with a weed killer and give it a few days to work before you patch. On the drives we have repaired, the areas where this step was skipped were the ones where green shoots appeared through the patch by the following spring.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Step 4: Fill Any Deep or Wide Cracks First</h3>
        <p>
          Gator patch is a surface product. It will not fill a half-inch crack to any depth; it just bridges the top and the bridge fails. Any crack in the network wider than about a quarter inch, or any that goes visibly deep, gets rubberised crack filler first, applied slightly below flush and left to cure per the label, typically overnight. If there is a genuine depression or a missing chunk, fill it with cold patch, compact it with a hand tamper or the end of a 4x4, and let it set up for a few days before patching over. Our piece on <a href="/cold-patch-asphalt/">cold patch asphalt</a> covers how to make that actually stay put.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Step 5: Trowel the Patch</h3>
        <p>
          Stir the patch thoroughly. Dump a workable amount onto the centre of the area and spread it outward with the trowel or squeegee, pressing firmly so it is forced down into the cracks rather than skating over them. You are aiming for a layer about an eighth of an inch thick over the cracked area, enough to bury the crack pattern completely, not a quarter-inch slab. If the pattern still telegraphs through after the first pass, that is normal; let it cure and apply a second thin coat rather than trying to do it all in one thick one. Thick applications shrink, crack as they dry, and take days to cure through.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Step 6: Feather the Edges</h3>
        <p>
          The transition from patch to sound asphalt is where a repair looks either professional or obvious. Work the edge out with the trowel held nearly flat so the patch thins to nothing over the last few inches. A hard edge catches snow shovel blades, shows through every coat of sealer afterwards, and tends to lift. Some people mist the edge with water and pass over it a final time to smooth it; that works, but go easy, because too much water thins the acrylic and weakens the bond right where you need it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Step 7: Let It Cure Fully</h3>
        <p>
          Most trowel-grade alligator patches are dry to walk on in a few hours and want 24 to 48 hours before being driven on and before sealing, in warm dry weather. Cool nights, humidity and shade extend that considerably. Sealing over patch that has not cured is the single most common reason an otherwise good repair goes wrong, because the sealer locks in the moisture the patch was still trying to release, and the result is a soft, grey, easily-scuffed area that never quite hardens.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Step 8: Seal</h3>
        <p>
          Once cured, seal the whole drive, or at minimum the patched area plus a generous margin. The patch will otherwise weather differently from the surrounding asphalt and stand out as a grey rectangle. Sealed, a well-done gator patch on a stable area is close to invisible, and the sealer protects the acrylic from UV, which is what eventually breaks it down. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-seal-a-driveway/">how to seal a driveway</a> covers the full process.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What to Expect</h3>
        <p>
          On a genuinely stable area, resealed annually or every other year, two to five years before it wants touching up is realistic, and we have seen patches go longer. On an area that moves underfoot, the crack pattern will reappear through the patch within a season. That is not the product failing; it is the product doing exactly what a surface treatment can do over a moving base, which is not very much. If that happens, stop buying patch and move to the next option.
        </p>

        <h2 id="cut-out" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Option B: Cut Out and Replace
        </h2>

        <p>
          This is the correct repair for alligatoring that is moving, sinking, or has a known cause in the base. It is also the only one of the four that actually addresses the problem rather than the symptom. The failed asphalt is cut out to sound material, the base is dug down, dried, rebuilt and compacted, and new asphalt is placed and compacted flush with the surrounding surface.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Process</h3>
        <p>
          Mark out a rectangle that extends at least six inches into sound asphalt on every side. Saw-cut the perimeter with a diamond blade so the edges are clean and vertical; a ragged edge is where the new material separates from the old. Break out and remove the alligatored asphalt. Then excavate the base to at least six inches, and further if it is wet or soft. If the subgrade is saturated, that is your cause, and it either needs to dry out or you need to address why water is getting there. Place crushed stone in lifts of no more than three inches and compact each one with a plate compactor, not a hand tamper; the base failed because it was not stiff enough, and hand tamping does not produce a stiff base. Bring it up to about two to three inches below the finished surface.
        </p>

        <p>
          Tack the cut edges with an asphalt emulsion or tack coat so the new material bonds to the old. Then fill with hot-mix asphalt if you can get it, placed slightly proud and compacted with a plate compactor or roller until it is flush. Hot-mix is dramatically better than cold patch for this because it compacts to a genuinely dense, stable layer, but it has to be laid within an hour or two of leaving the plant and most plants will not sell you a wheelbarrow-load. That practical constraint is why cut-and-replace is usually a contractor job, and why our honest advice in the <a href="/diy-vs-professional-driveway-sealing/">DIY versus professional</a> debate shifts toward the professional once you get past surface work.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you are doing it yourself with cold patch, use a quality high-performance cold patch rather than the cheapest bag on the shelf, place it in two-inch lifts, compact each lift thoroughly, and accept that it will take weeks of traffic to fully firm up and should not be sealed until it has. Cold patch in a properly rebuilt base works reasonably well. Cold patch dumped into a hole with a soft bottom is a repair you will do again next year.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cost</h3>
        <p>
          Contractors typically quote cut-and-replace repairs by the square foot, and the range we see is roughly 4 to 12 dollars per square foot depending on depth, access and how many small areas there are, with a minimum charge that makes tiny repairs expensive per foot. A single fifty-square-foot repair might land between 400 and 800 dollars. Several areas across a drive add up quickly, which is where the overlay conversation starts. General figures are in our guide to <a href="/asphalt-repair/">asphalt repair</a>.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Longevity</h3>
        <p>
          Done properly, a cut-and-replace repair should last as long as the surrounding drive, because it is new asphalt on a new base. The weak point is the seam, which wants crack-sealing when it opens, and it will open eventually. Sealcoating over the repair after it has cured for at least 30 days and preferably a full season helps it blend and protects the seam.
        </p>

        <h2 id="infrared-overlay" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Options C and D: Infrared, Overlay, Replacement
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Option C: Infrared Repair</h3>
        <p>
          Infrared asphalt repair uses a large heater to warm the existing surface to around 300 degrees Fahrenheit over a few minutes, at which point the old asphalt softens enough to be raked, mixed with a small amount of fresh hot-mix and a rejuvenating agent, and re-compacted. Because the repair fuses with the surrounding pavement rather than butting against it, there is no cold seam, and the result is close to seamless. It is fast and it is comparatively tidy.
        </p>

        <p>
          The honest limitation is that infrared works on the asphalt, not the base. It is excellent for alligatoring caused by aged brittle asphalt over a base that is still sound, and for surface depressions, and it does a good job blending a repair into the surrounding drive. It does nothing for a soft, wet base, and infrared repairs over failed base alligator again within a year or two. Ask the contractor to probe the base before recommending it. Cost is typically in the same range as cut-and-replace or slightly under, and availability depends on whether a contractor in your area owns the equipment.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Option D: Overlay</h3>
        <p>
          An overlay is a new layer of hot-mix asphalt, usually one and a half to two inches, laid over the existing drive after the worst areas have been repaired and the surface has been cleaned and tack-coated. It gives you a new surface at a fraction of the cost of full replacement, and on a drive with widespread block cracking or ravelling it is often the right call. Typical pricing runs 3 to 7 dollars per square foot depending on region and thickness, and our <a href="/driveway-resurfacing-cost/">driveway resurfacing cost</a> guide goes into the variables.
        </p>

        <p>
          The caveat with alligatoring specifically is that an overlay over unrepaired alligator cracking will reflect the cracks through within two to four years. The base is still moving; it just has more asphalt on top of it to crack. A reputable contractor will insist on cutting out and rebuilding the alligatored areas before overlaying, and if they do not mention it, ask. Also be aware that an overlay raises the surface, so it needs to be tapered at the garage, the road and any drains, and it is generally only viable once.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Option E: Full Replacement</h3>
        <p>
          When alligatoring covers a large proportion of the drive, when there is general settlement, or when the drive has already been overlaid once, full-depth replacement is the answer. Everything comes out, the base is rebuilt to a proper depth, ideally six to eight inches of compacted stone on a stable subgrade, and new asphalt goes down in one or two lifts. This is expensive, and current figures are in our <a href="/asphalt-driveway-cost/">asphalt driveway cost</a> guide, but it is the only option that resets the clock completely. If the original drive alligatored at ten years because the base was thin, make sure the new one gets a properly specified base, because it is the base, not the asphalt, that determines how long the next one lasts.
        </p>

        <h2 id="sealing" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          7. Why Sealer Alone Fails, and Sealing After Repair
        </h2>

        <p>
          It is worth being very clear on this because the marketing on some sealer pails is not. Driveway sealer, whether coal tar, asphalt emulsion or acrylic, is a thin protective coating. When dry it is a fraction of a millimetre thick. Its job is to keep UV and water off the asphalt binder and to restore a uniform black colour. It has no structural strength, it cannot bridge a crack that is moving, and it adds nothing to the load-bearing capacity of the pavement. Sealer is sunscreen. Alligator cracking is a broken bone.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What Happens When You Seal Over It</h3>
        <p>
          The sealer flows into the cracks and, for a few weeks, the area looks uniformly black and the problem appears solved. Then the pieces move under the first car, the sealer bridging each crack splits, and water gets in exactly as before. Worse, the sealer film across the loose pieces is now being flexed constantly, and because there is more sealer pooled in the cracks than on the flat, those thick pools cure slowly and stay soft. Within a season the alligatored area is grey again, the sealer over it has cracked into the same pattern, and it starts to peel at the edges of each piece because the bond to loose, dirty, oxidised fragments was never good. It is the most common cause of <a href="/driveway-sealer-peeling/">driveway sealer peeling</a> in a localised area.
        </p>

        <p>
          You have then spent the money on sealer, the area is no better, and you have added a layer of failing coating that has to be dealt with before a proper repair. Our guide on whether <a href="/can-you-seal-a-cracked-asphalt-driveway/">you can seal a cracked asphalt driveway</a> draws the line: hairline and shrinkage cracks, yes with filler first; alligatoring, not until it has been patched.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Sealing After a Repair</h3>
        <p>
          After a gator patch, wait for the patch's full cure, 24 to 48 hours minimum in good weather, and be patient if the weather has been cool. After cold patch, give it at least two to four weeks of traffic to compact and release its solvents; seal too soon and the solvents soften the sealer from below and it never hardens. After hot-mix, whether a cut-and-replace, infrared or overlay, most contractors recommend waiting a minimum of 30 days and many prefer 90 days to a full season so the fresh asphalt can oxidise slightly and the sealer can bond. Our guide on <a href="/when-should-you-seal-a-new-asphalt-driveway/">when to seal a new asphalt driveway</a> explains why rushing this backfires.
        </p>

        <p>
          When you do seal, use a decent product and apply it thin. A quality asphalt emulsion or a coal-tar-alternative sealer from our <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">best asphalt driveway sealer</a> round-up, two thin coats rather than one thick one, and consider a sand additive over the patched areas to improve traction and hide texture differences. Then keep it up. Alligatoring is fundamentally a water problem, and the drives that never develop it are the ones where cracks were filled and sealed before water had years to work on the base.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Sealing over it and hoping</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Sealer hides alligator cracking for about a month. Then it cracks in the same pattern, peels, and you have a worse surface to repair than you started with.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Patching an area that moves underfoot</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Gator patch bridges cracks on a stable surface. On a rocking one it splits along the same lines within a season. If it moves, it needs to come out.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Applying the patch too thick</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A quarter-inch slab of acrylic patch shrinks, cracks as it dries, and takes days to cure through. Two thin coats always beat one thick one.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Ignoring the water source</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">If a downspout or a sloping lawn is feeding water under that corner of the drive, every repair there will fail until the water is dealt with.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Sealing before the repair has cured</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Trapped moisture or solvent under sealer leaves a soft grey patch that scuffs and never hardens. Wait the full cure, and then some in cool weather.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What causes alligator cracking in an asphalt driveway?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Alligator cracking is fatigue failure. The asphalt flexes under load more than it can tolerate, either because the base beneath it has softened or washed out, or because the asphalt itself has aged and gone brittle. The interconnected pattern is the surface breaking up because it is no longer properly supported.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you just seal over alligator cracks?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">No. Sealer is a thin surface coating, typically less than the thickness of a credit card when dry. It cannot bridge cracks or add strength. Over unpatched alligatoring it fills the cracks, hides them for a season, then flexes, cracks and peels as the loose pieces underneath keep moving.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best product for small alligator cracked areas?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A trowel-grade alligator patch such as Latex-ite Gator Patch or a comparable fibre-reinforced acrylic or emulsion patch. These are designed to be spread across a spider-webbed area in a thin layer, bridging the cracks and locking the surface pieces together. They work on stable areas under about 20 square feet.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does an alligator patch last?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">On an area that is stable underfoot, two to five years is realistic before it needs attention, and longer if it is sealed over annually. On an area that is soft or rocks when you step on it, expect it to crack through within a season because the underlying movement has not been addressed.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to repair alligator cracks?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A DIY gator patch for a few small areas runs roughly 30 to 80 dollars in materials. A contractor cut-and-replace repair typically lands between 4 and 12 dollars per square foot depending on depth and access. Infrared repair is similar or slightly less. A full overlay or replacement is priced by the whole drive, commonly 3 to 7 dollars per square foot for an overlay and more for full-depth work.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should I wait to sealcoat after patching?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Follow the patch manufacturer's cure time, which is usually 24 to 48 hours for trowel-grade patch in warm dry weather and considerably longer in cool or humid conditions. Cold patch asphalt should be driven on for a few weeks to compact and shed its solvents before it is sealed. Sealing too early traps solvent and the sealer stays soft.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I tell alligator cracking from block cracking?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Block cracking forms large roughly square pieces, typically a foot to several feet across, spread over the whole drive. It comes from shrinkage of aged asphalt and is not a base problem. Alligator cracking forms small tight interconnected pieces, usually a few inches across, in localised areas, and the pieces often rock or sit low.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">When is alligator cracking beyond repair?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">When it covers more than roughly a quarter of the drive, when the affected areas are sinking or pumping water when you stand on them, or when the drive is more than 20 to 25 years old and cracking everywhere at once. At that point patching is money spent delaying the inevitable and an overlay or replacement is the honest answer.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

