        </ul>

        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 rounded-2xl p-8 text-gray-200">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Alligator cracking is the asphalt reporting a problem underneath it. Small, stable areas can be bridged with a trowel-grade gator patch and sealed, and that repair will hold for years if the area is not moving. Anything that rocks, sinks or holds water needs the failed material cut out and the base rebuilt, which is usually a contractor job.
          </p>
          <p class="m-0">
            Whatever you do, do not pour sealer over it. Sealer protects sound asphalt; it does not repair broken asphalt, and over alligatoring it fails in months and makes the eventual repair harder. Fix the cause, patch or replace the surface, let it cure, and then seal.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
